From 9b2c3ef10dbe5ee8054ceaa639009814e1dda622 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:51:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: do not decode the enhancement layer of a MEL
 stream

A profile 7 stream decodes its enhancement layer, a second HEVC decode of
usually 1080p 10-bit, for every picture. Where its RPUs say that the layer
carries no residual (MEL), the bridge never draws it: libplacebo's mapping
of the metadata (pl_map_dovi_metadata()) has nlq_active false and the
layer is left out before anything is drawn, and since 0b7b850 it is not
even imported. The decode was work, memory bandwidth and VAAPI surfaces for
an invisible layer.

What MEL is (dvbridge_mel.h): an RPU has no residual exactly when that
mapping draws none, that is when its residual is disabled
(disable_residual_flag), or when its NLQ is the linear dead zone with, in
each of the three components, offset 0, vdr_in_max 1 (1 << coef_log2_denom),
slope 0 and threshold 0 (pl_avdovi_nlq_is_trivial(); also Kodi's own MEL of
strDVELType). With a slope and a threshold of 0 the residual is 0 whatever
the samples of the layer are. Any other NLQ is FEL, also in a picture whose
layer happens to sit at its offset everywhere (the FEL test stream has 332
such pictures): the RPU decides, never the samples.

The module reads the RPU of each base packet before it is decoded, as
FFmpeg's HEVC decoder takes it from the access unit (the last unit of type
62 after the first, of layer 0 and temporal id 0) and as ff_dovi_rpu_parse()
reads it as far as the NLQ: emulation prevention, fixed and floating point
coefficients, polynomial and MMR curves, the mappings that a later RPU uses
again (use_prev_vdr_rpu) and their loss on a failure or a flush. What it
cannot read is taken as an RPU that can carry a residual. The same in
single-track streams (the RPU in the access unit of the base packet) and in
streams of two tracks (the RPU that CDVLayerMerge puts there).

The decision, access unit by access unit in decoding order:
- the layer stops being decoded only at one of its random-access pictures
  whose RPU says MEL: no picture of a group that is decoded loses a
  reference. The decoder is given its end first, so that it gives the
  pictures it holds;
- while it is not decoded, an RPU that can carry a residual starts it again
  at its next random-access picture, as after a seek: the decoder is flushed
  and the leading pictures of that picture go out without the layer. When
  that RPU came within a group of pictures, its pictures until then go out
  without their residual (counted, as a missing layer is), and the layer is
  decoded for good in that stream: its RPUs change within groups, where
  leaving it out loses residuals. So a FEL picture is never shown without
  its residual longer than until the next random-access picture of the
  layer, and that at most once a stream;
- an access unit without an RPU of its own changes nothing; a seek or a
  resynchronization starts the layer at a random-access picture again, and
  that picture decides.

An access unit that is not decoded leaves a mark of its presentation time
(by picture order, or by the times of the two tracks, as a decoded one is
paired) with the RPU of its access unit. Its base picture is answered with
a frame without a picture that carries what the enhancement decoder would
have attached (dvbridge_fel_take() 3, or 4 for a picture that needs its
layer): that RPU as coded and the metadata the base decoder made of the same
RPU, which Kodi hands to the module for each base picture
(dvbridge_fel_base_metadata()). UseLayerDVMetadata() then gives the picture
the metadata it has when the layer is decoded, also where the layers are
coded in different picture order (the test streams: 2523 of 2876 pictures
take another RPU than the one of their packet). A picture whose RPU came
with a base picture that is still to come waits for it (answer 0).

Kodi marks such a picture (VideoPicture::doviLayerNotDecoded). The renderer
draws it as a picture whose layer is not drawn (layerWithoutResidual, as for
a decoded MEL layer): the same way of drawing, step-down level, kept frame
and check, and the same call into the bridge. The Dolby Vision page says
"BL + EL (MEL, not decoded)" (Estuary 31962); the log says once a stream
"DV FEL: enhancement layer not decoded: MEL", when the layer is decoded
again, and how many access units were and were not decoded at the end.

Tested on the host (software decode, the module itself, the FEL test
streams of one and two tracks):
- mel_need_test: the reading of RPUs made bit by bit and the decision, 122
  checks; 26 mutations of dvbridge_mel.h, each caught;
- mel_test with the RPUs of the test streams rewritten (their NLQ, or the
  residual disabled; the rest bit for bit, the CRC made again), each read by
  FFmpeg's own parser: the module reads every RPU as libplacebo's rule reads
  FFmpeg's parse of it (as is, MEL, disabled, and MEL but an offset, a slope
  or vdr_in_max 2). As is (FEL), every picture is the same as without the
  rule: pairing, enhancement picture, metadata. With MEL RPUs no enhancement
  access unit is decoded, and every picture goes out with the metadata it
  has when the layer is decoded, in one track and in two; MEL, FEL from a
  random-access picture and MEL again: decoded exactly there; FEL from
  within a group: wanted until the next random-access picture, then decoded
  for good; seeks in both;
- the earlier tests of the module (fel_rpu_test, fel_tracks_test), as before.
Work of the enhancement decoder in software on the host, the MEL test
stream of one track (2876 pictures): 2876 access units, 49.9 s; with the
rule, none. Not run on a GPU, nor with a MEL disc: no such stream here.
